1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,750
HDX is not a central data lake. Think of it
as a federation:

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:09,275
a central Discovery and Governance entity
indexes what's available across the network,

3
00:00:09,425 --> 00:00:13,300
so anyone can find it — but the data itself
never moves there.

4
00:00:13,450 --> 00:00:18,763
Every participating institution runs its own
federated node, keeping its own data and

5
00:00:18,912 --> 00:00:23,600
services, and operating its own control
plane for access control and audit.

6
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:28,375
Data is discovered centrally. It only ever
leaves its source with consent.

7
00:00:28,925 --> 00:00:31,125
Here's that federation in practice.

8
00:00:31,275 --> 00:00:36,712
Every card is an independent institution —
Node A, Node B, City General Hospital — each

9
00:00:36,862 --> 00:00:40,238
with its own infrastructure and its own
control plane.

10
00:00:40,387 --> 00:00:44,075
Only metadata and pointers join the shared
federated index;

11
00:00:44,225 --> 00:00:47,412
the underlying records stay exactly where
they are.

12
00:00:47,962 --> 00:00:51,962
Opening a node shows exactly what it
contributes to the network:

13
00:00:52,112 --> 00:00:54,425
the datasets and services it exposes.

14
00:00:54,575 --> 00:00:59,700
Node A here hosts seven datasets, spanning
diabetes, oncology, cardiovascular, and

15
00:00:59,850 --> 00:01:05,162
imaging data, plus one compute service — all
discoverable, none of it copied anywhere

16
00:01:05,312 --> 00:01:07,512
else.

17
00:01:08,062 --> 00:01:13,250
This is the Data Plane — every dataset from
every node, in one federated catalogue.

18
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:18,587
A consumer can filter by node or by track,
and scan standards and access tiers at a

19
00:01:18,738 --> 00:01:20,938
glance without leaving this page.

20
00:01:21,487 --> 00:01:26,300
Opening a dataset surfaces its full metadata
before anyone requests anything:

21
00:01:26,450 --> 00:01:29,512
standards used, access tier, tags, record
counts.

22
00:01:29,662 --> 00:01:35,100
This one — the Diabetic Glaucoma Screening
Cohort — follows the mCXDE diabetes profile,

23
00:01:35,250 --> 00:01:37,938
which is worth seeing as more than a label.

24
00:01:38,487 --> 00:01:43,987
Standards like mCXDE aren't bolted onto a
single dataset — they're part of the
platform.

25
00:01:44,137 --> 00:01:48,012
HDX renders the whole profile as an
interactive concept graph.

26
00:01:48,162 --> 00:01:51,350
Overview returns to the top-level clinical
domains;

27
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:54,500
Fit graph and the zoom controls adjust the
view.

28
00:01:55,050 --> 00:02:00,050
Each chip in the legend is a clinical domain
in the model — Disease, Assessment,

29
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:02,400
Treatment, and so on.

30
00:02:02,550 --> 00:02:07,925
Clicking one hides or shows it, which is how
you narrow a dense graph down to just the

31
00:02:08,075 --> 00:02:10,275
part you care about.

32
00:02:10,825 --> 00:02:14,575
Clicking a domain node expands it into its
own detail graph.

33
00:02:14,725 --> 00:02:19,162
Clicking a leaf concept goes one level
deeper — opening its actual FHIR

34
00:02:19,312 --> 00:02:23,438
StructureDefinition next to a worked example
Bundle, side by side.

35
00:02:23,987 --> 00:02:29,050
Services are catalogued the same way
datasets are — by category, by hosting node,

36
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:34,575
and by which datasets each one is registered
to operate on. This is the Service Plane.

37
00:02:35,125 --> 00:02:39,625
Every dataset carries a consent gate. A non-
owner requests access here;

38
00:02:39,775 --> 00:02:44,337
the owning institution — or an admin —
approves it before anything moves.

39
00:02:44,487 --> 00:02:49,425
Once granted, a download streams from the
source node's own file server over an

40
00:02:49,575 --> 00:02:53,262
authenticated call. There's never a public
link handed out.

41
00:02:53,812 --> 00:02:59,250
Every access request, whether it's one you
made or one waiting on something you own, is

42
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:01,600
tracked in one place.

43
00:03:01,750 --> 00:03:06,687
Approving access to an entire node
automatically grants everything hosted under

44
00:03:06,837 --> 00:03:09,837
it — no need to approve each dataset one by
one.

45
00:03:10,387 --> 00:03:14,325
Requesting a service works identically to
requesting a dataset.

46
00:03:14,475 --> 00:03:20,037
Once you're consented, actually invoking the
service routes your data through the control

47
00:03:20,187 --> 00:03:24,250
plane's secure enclave — the chokepoint
every job passes through.

48
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:28,650
The service itself only ever sees the one
dataset it's been granted.

49
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:31,737
It never gets a raw path into a node's
storage.

50
00:03:32,287 --> 00:03:35,412
Services with a live try-it flow show this
button.

51
00:03:35,562 --> 00:03:40,812
Glaucoma Detection is one of them — click
through to open its cohort screening demo.

52
00:03:41,362 --> 00:03:45,487
This is where a consumer routes a specific
dataset to the service.

53
00:03:45,637 --> 00:03:50,887
We've already pasted a dataset ID from the
exchange — ds-diabetic-glaucoma, the same

54
00:03:51,037 --> 00:03:55,287
cohort we looked at earlier — so the next
step is simply to load it.

55
00:03:55,837 --> 00:03:59,400
With the cohort loaded, running the service
is one click.

56
00:03:59,550 --> 00:04:04,237
Every patient in the cohort gets sent
through the glaucoma screening model.

57
00:04:04,787 --> 00:04:09,787
And here are the results — one row per
patient, positive and negative screenings

58
00:04:09,937 --> 00:04:12,137
side by side.

59
00:04:12,287 --> 00:04:17,162
Clicking any row expands its fundus
photograph, the model's stated conclusion,

60
00:04:17,312 --> 00:04:22,812
and the raw FHIR input and output bundles
that were actually exchanged for that
patient.

61
00:04:22,962 --> 00:04:25,162
This is the full round trip:

62
00:04:25,312 --> 00:04:30,437
a consented dataset, routed through the
control plane, processed by a service, and

63
00:04:30,587 --> 00:04:33,275
returned as a structured, auditable result.

64
00:04:33,825 --> 00:04:37,700
Everything a provider does starts here — one
of three wizards:

65
00:04:37,850 --> 00:04:40,350
onboard a node, a dataset, or a service.

66
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:45,962
This is how an institution — say, ICMR —
joins the federation in the first place.

67
00:04:46,112 --> 00:04:48,550
They register their basic details here.

68
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:52,950
The request sits pending until an HDX admin
reviews and approves it;

69
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:58,600
approval is what grants the institution the
node-owner role and a control-plane identity

70
00:04:58,750 --> 00:05:00,950
scoped to their own node.

71
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:06,875
Once an institution is an approved node
owner, publishing a dataset is a short
wizard:

72
00:05:07,025 --> 00:05:12,150
basic info, the standards and access tier it
follows, and an optional sample file.

73
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:17,175
As soon as it's published, it's discoverable
in the Data Plane we saw earlier.

74
00:05:17,725 --> 00:05:22,975
Onboarding a service follows the same shape
— register it against your node, declare

75
00:05:23,125 --> 00:05:27,625
which datasets it's permitted to operate on,
and attach an OpenAPI spec.

76
00:05:27,775 --> 00:05:32,962
From that point on, it's discoverable and
requestable in the Service Plane, exactly

77
00:05:33,112 --> 00:05:36,237
like the Glaucoma Detection service we just
tried.
